F1 REPRODUCTION — is §22.1's root cause ("a .metal edit does not reach the
binary because of include_str!") true?

Three checks, all through the ordinary build path. No env var, no --features, no
touch, no clean.

--- 1. cargo tracks the edit -------------------------------------------------
$ cp crates/kernels/src/attention.metal /tmp/attention.metal.orig
$ python3   # insert `if (tid < 256u) { return; }` at the top of
            # attention_prefill_flash_body (§10's exact arm)
$ cargo build --release -p kaplumba-engine --tests
   Compiling kaplumba-kernels v0.1.0 (/Users/zayaantaha/coding/kaplumba/crates/kernels)
    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 11.92s

So cargo recompiled the crate that `include_str!`s the file. The mechanism §22.1
names does not apply.

--- 2. the arm runs, and it collapses ----------------------------------------
$ scripts/gpuq try -- cargo test --release --test attention_qk_mma_bench \
      attention_arm_throughput_at_prefill_geometry -- --nocapture --exact
  flash bq1    0.16 ms  [0.16 0.18]  3371.02 TFLOP/s      <- 8192 keys
--- full    32q/4kv/d512 (NSG=8) keys=24576 queries=1024 (1649.27 GFLOP/iter) ---
  flash bq1    0.18 ms  [0.16 0.23]  9158.37 TFLOP/s      <- 24576 keys
test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; ... finished in 47.88s

§10 recorded 964.79 ms for this arm. Here it is 0.18 ms: a 5300x collapse,
through the ordinary build path, no rebuild trickery.

--- 3. restore, same command, kernel is back ---------------------------------
$ cp /tmp/attention.metal.orig crates/kernels/src/attention.metal
$ cargo build --release -p kaplumba-engine --tests
    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 11.73s
$ scripts/gpuq try -- cargo test --release --test attention_qk_mma_bench \
      attention_arm_throughput_at_prefill_geometry -- --nocapture --exact
  flash bq1  288.78 ms  [287.56 293.10]    1.90 TFLOP/s
  flash bq1  938.15 ms  [938.14 942.58]    1.76 TFLOP/s
  flash bq1  237.53 ms  [237.48 238.42]    1.74 TFLOP/s
  flash bq1 3728.72 ms  [3713.98 3733.57]  1.77 TFLOP/s
test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; ... finished in 87.87s

--- 4. and a BROKEN .metal fails loudly, it does not fall back to a stale one -
$ python3   # insert `this is not valid metal at all;`
$ scripts/gpuq try -- cargo test --release ... > /tmp/brokenbuild.log 2>&1
raw rc=101
grep -cE "flash bq1" /tmp/brokenbuild.log   ->  0        (no timing printed at all)
$ cargo test --release ... 2>&1 | grep -E "flash bq1|test result"
test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 1 filtered out
grep-pipeline rc=0

Note the last line: through a grep-filtered view the exit status is grep's, i.e.
0, on a FAILED build. That is §10.3's recorded process bug and it is the whole of
why "the build returns 0" cannot establish anything on its own — but note what
it does and does not produce: a build failure produces NO timing line, not a
baseline-looking one. So §10's numbers are not build failures.

CONCLUSION

* §10's arm is a genuinely empty kernel (tid < 256 with 256 threads/group) and
  it reads ~0.2 ms. F1's *conclusion* stands.
* The arm reaches the binary by the ordinary build path. F1's stated *root
  cause* — `include_str!` hiding the edit — is wrong.
* §10's table also contains an arm that DID move (the 3 000 000-iteration
  threadgroup spin, 4 828.96 ms against a 958.39 ms baseline). An added spin
  cannot move a kernel that is not executing, so the edits were reaching the
  shader in that run too. Both facts cannot hold of one binary.
* The reconciliation is §10's own method: "tree restored with `git checkout`
  after every arm". `git checkout` rewrites the file with a fresh mtime, and
  cargo's freshness test for an `include_str!` input is mtime-based, so
  edit -> build -> run -> checkout -> edit -> build -> run has a window in which
  the next arm's build compiles the RESTORED source. That produces exactly §10's
  signature: most arms at the baseline, one arm outside the window that moves.
  It is a build-freshness race, not an include_str! limitation.
